[1mDESCRIPTION[0m[m
       [4mzip[24m is a compression and file packaging utility for Unix, VMS, MSDOS, OS/2, Windows 9x/NT/XP, Minix, Atari, Macintosh, Amiga, and Acorn RISC OS.  It is analogous to a combination of the Unix commands [4mtar[24m(1) and [4mcompress[24m(1) and is compatible[m
       with PKZIP (Phil Katz's ZIP for MSDOS systems).[m
[m
       A companion program ([4munzip[24m(1)) unpacks [4mzip[24m archives.  The [4mzip[24m and [4munzip[24m(1) programs can work with archives produced by PKZIP (supporting most PKZIP features up to PKZIP version 4.6), and PKZIP and PKUNZIP can work with archives produced  by[m
       [4mzip[24m (with some exceptions, notably streamed archives, but recent changes in the zip file standard may facilitate better compatibility).  [4mzip[24m version 3.0 is compatible with PKZIP 2.04 and also supports the Zip64 extensions of PKZIP 4.5 which[m
       allow archives as well as files to exceed the previous 2 GB limit (4 GB in some cases).  [4mzip[24m also now supports [1mbzip2[0m compression if the [1mbzip2[0m library is included when [4mzip[24m is compiled.  Note that PKUNZIP 1.10 cannot extract files produced by[m
       PKZIP 2.04 or [4mzip[24m [4m3.0[24m. You must use PKUNZIP 2.04g or [4munzip[24m [4m5.0p1[24m (or later versions) to extract them.[m
[m
       See the [1mEXAMPLES[0m section at the bottom of this page for examples of some typical uses of [4mzip[24m.[m
[m
       [1mLarge[0m [1mArchives[0m [1mand[0m [1mZip64.[0m  [4mzip[24m automatically uses the Zip64 extensions when files larger than 4 GB are added to an archive, an archive containing Zip64 entries is updated (if the resulting archive still needs Zip64), the size of the archive[m
       will exceed 4 GB, or when the number of entries in the archive will exceed about 64K.  Zip64 is also used for archives streamed from standard input as the size of such archives are not known in advance, but the option [1m-fz-[0m can  be  used  to[m
       force [4mzip[24m to create PKZIP 2 compatible archives (as long as Zip64 extensions are not needed).  You must use a PKZIP 4.5 compatible unzip, such as [4munzip[24m [4m6.0[24m or later, to extract files using the Zip64 extensions.[m
[m
       In addition, streamed archives, entries encrypted with standard encryption, or split archives created with the pause option may not be compatible with PKZIP as data descriptors are used and PKZIP at the time of this writing does not support[m
       data descriptors (but recent changes in the PKWare published zip standard now include some support for the data descriptor format [4mzip[24m uses).[m
[m
       [1mMac[0m [1mOS[0m [1mX.[0m  Though previous Mac versions had their own [4mzip[24m port, [4mzip[24m supports Mac OS X as part of the Unix port and most Unix features apply.  References to "MacOS" below generally refer to MacOS versions older than OS X.  Support  for  some[m
       Mac OS features in the Unix Mac OS X port, such as resource forks, is expected in the next [4mzip[24m release.[m
[m
       For a brief help on [4mzip[24m and [4munzip[24m, run each without specifying any parameters on the command line.[m
[m
[1mUSE[0m[m
       The program is useful for packaging a set of files for distribution; for archiving files; and for saving disk space by temporarily compressing unused files or directories.[m
[m
       The [4mzip[24m program puts one or more compressed files into a single [4mzip[24m archive, along with information about the files (name, path, date, time of last modification, protection, and check information to verify file integrity).  An entire direc‐[m
       tory structure can be packed into a [4mzip[24m archive with a single command.  Compression ratios of 2:1 to 3:1 are common for text files.  [4mzip[24m has one compression method (deflation) and can also store files without compression.  (If [1mbzip2[0m support[m
       is  added,  [4mzip[24m  can  also compress using [1mbzip2[0m compression, but such entries require a reasonably modern unzip to decompress.  When [1mbzip2[0m compression is selected, it replaces deflation as the default method.)  [4mzip[24m automatically chooses the[m
       better of the two (deflation or store or, if [1mbzip2[0m is selected, [1mbzip2[0m or store) for each file to be compressed.[m
[m
       [1mCommand[0m [1mformat.[0m  The basic command format is[m
[m
              [1mzip[0m options archive inpath inpath ...[m
[m
       where [1marchive[0m is a new or existing [4mzip[24m archive and [1minpath[0m is a directory or file path optionally including wildcards.  When given the name of an existing [4mzip[24m archive, [4mzip[24m will replace identically named entries in the [4mzip[24m  archive  (matching[m
       the relative names as stored in the archive) or add entries for new names.  For example, if [4mfoo.zip[24m exists and contains [4mfoo/file1[24m and [4mfoo/file2[24m, and the directory [4mfoo[24m contains the files [4mfoo/file1[24m and [4mfoo/file3[24m, then:[m
[m
              zip -r foo.zip foo[m
[m
       or more concisely[m
[m
              zip -r foo foo[m
[m
